    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "I love that question, because developers are basically professional tool collectors. We see a shiny new package and think, “This will save me a week,” and then six months later we’re maintaining it. So what counts as using the platform in practice?",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host2",
    "text": "It might mean relying on browser features before adding a JavaScript library, using standard operating-system tools rather than shipping a custom utility, or choosing built-in language capabilities over another dependency. The trade-off is real: platform features can reduce bundle size, installation friction, and security exposure. But they can also vary across versions, be harder to discover, or lack the polished API a team wants.",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "So this isn’t “never use a library,” it’s “know what you’re buying.” A dependency can be the right call, especially when it saves time or handles tricky edge cases. But before adding one, maybe ask: Is this solving a gap in the platform, or just a gap in my memory?",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host2",
    "text": "Exactly. And there’s a team-level implication too. Dependencies don’t just add code; they add upgrade work, compatibility questions, and another place where bugs can enter. On the other hand, a well-maintained open-source project can give a small team capabilities it couldn’t reasonably build itself. The useful habit is evaluating the whole cost, not treating “built in” or “third-party” as automatically superior.",
